    "turn_idx": 5,
    "turn_text": "Well, yeah, the second part. So this is done with a vendor that the state of Florida contracted with and the vendor got written consent forms from everybody that was transported also provided a packet that had a map of Martha's vineyard numbers on the vineyard for social services as well as for the state of Massachusetts. So there was no doubt that that's where they were going. And in fact, when they were getting ready to do the transport, there were some of the migrants who said, you know what, we don't want to go to Massachusetts, and so they ended up not going. So it was purely voluntary, and it was that, and here's the thing. The policy that we have is simply, I would like to see President Trump's border policies re-instituted. I mean, most of the people coming across the border illegally are making effectively bogus asylum claims. So, if they're making asylum claims that we know 95 plus percent are not going to be valid or 90 percent, they should wait in Mexico, let that claim be adjudicated. If they have a valid claim, then obviously, you know, they're entitled to use that law to come in. But instead, what Joe Biden's done is he's basically opened the border. You have people pouring in 40 percent of the folks, because I've had people on the southern border helping Texas last year and then in our conversations. you know between 30 and 40 percent have said Florida is one of their preferred destinations and so we obviously are not a sanctuary state we just can't absorb it's not the way you do policy to just have floods of people coming in and so what by then so our view is is you know we want to divert to sanctuary states who have said that's their policy so that's why we got the 12 million dollars in the budget. You know, we've also done things that haven't gotten as much press like we've had interdictions in the panhandle, we've seized narcotics, we've been able to bring some of the, I guess their coyotes or smugglers up on charges, but that's kind of a onesie-toozy thing. If you're able to get into a sanctuary jurisdiction, the hope is is that they would stick because they'd be able to have benefits and whatnot. But also, I do believe that the people that proclaim to be sanctuary jurisdictions are the ones advocating for this open border policy, and yet a lot of them seem to not want to have to deal with any of the consequences of their own policies And I think what happened in Martha's Vineyard is an example of that. I mean, they had proclaimed to be a sanctuary area. They had said that they wanted to be a hub. I think Ted Cruz last year had said that they should be the place and these politicians saying, yeah, we want to be the place for that. Okay. So then when you have just 50 of them, Not only did they not be did not act as a sanctuary they called in the National Guard and they deported them off the island the next day So it just shows they want to impose burdens on the rest of the country, but they don't want to do it So ideally you wouldn't have buses going from El Paso to New York like you have you wouldn't have Greg Abbott doing buses or Florida doing any of this stuff you would just recognize that the policy of Biden has failed Change it and then a lot of these problems would go away",
